The P1 Gefion Sandbox provides on-demand access to 1–2 H100 GPUs on
[Gefion](https://dcai.dk/gefion) for iterative development, testing, and small-scale pilots. It is
designed as a stepping stone — enabling a seamless transition from early experiments into larger
production workloads on full 8-GPU nodes (e.g., for NNF voucher projects).

Detailed internal documentation is maintained by the DCAI team at
[guide.gefion.dcai.dk](https://guide.gefion.dcai.dk). It is currently only accessible via the
Citrix Gateway or via Teleport with Web Application Access (though the latter has not been tested
in depth yet).

## Getting Access

### Talk to the compute coordinator

Onboarding starts with a short conversation. Fill in the
[workload survey](https://survey.starostka.io/compute) to tell the P1 compute coordinator about
your needs, expectations, and workload — for the sandbox this also helps us plan the step up to
full production nodes if your project scales.

### Sign-up Form

Before accessing the P1 Gefion sandbox, you must be a P1 affiliate. Sign in to
[your P1 profile](https://www.aicentre.dk/p1adb) (also reachable via **Update Profile** in the
header), update your details, and submit a request to become an affiliate member — a P1 staff
member will review and approve.

Once your affiliation is approved, fill in the sandbox sign-up form below. A DCAI team member will
then contact you to get you set up with a DCAI username and initial-setup-password.

<EmbedForm
  title="P1 Gefion Sandbox Sign-up Form"
  src="https://forms.office.com/Pages/ResponsePage.aspx?id=I_FR8s7JjkSSdzS7KFkR2cHxzPsQ0cdIuQChdDR2mhJUNVdUNU9KUU9SSFZXNjVDWExTVEFPMElWTi4u&embed=true"
  minHeight="1200px"
/>

### Who's eligible?

**Requirements:** PhD or higher (exceptions may apply), valid Danish university email, and
registered P1 affiliation.

## Getting Started

### How to connect

Gefion uses [Teleport](https://goteleport.com/) for secure SSH access. Install the
[Teleport CLI client (`tsh`)](https://goteleport.com/docs/connect-your-client/tsh/) and
authenticate:

```bash
tsh login --proxy secureremote.dcai.dk --user <username>
```

Then add the following to your `~/.ssh/config`:

```text file=~/.ssh/config
Host gefion
  Port 3022
  User <username>
  HostName slurm-login-01.hpc.ite.dcai.dk
  ProxyCommand tsh proxy ssh --cluster=secureremote.dcai.dk --proxy=secureremote.dcai.dk:443 %r@%h:%p
  CertificateFile ~/.tsh/keys/secureremote.dcai.dk/<username>-ssh/secureremote.dcai.dk-cert.pub
  ConnectTimeout 30
  ControlMaster no
  ControlPersist no
  IdentityFile ~/.tsh/keys/secureremote.dcai.dk/<username>
  UserKnownHostsFile ~/.tsh/known_hosts
```

Connect with:

```bash
ssh gefion
```

#### Visual Studio Code

With the SSH config in place you can use the
[VS Code Remote – SSH extension](https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/remote/ssh) to connect to
`gefion` via the command palette (Ctrl+Shift+P → "Remote-SSH: Connect to Host…").

### How to transfer data

Use `rsync` for source code and small datasets, and [rclone](https://rclone.org/) for large
datasets — both work over the Teleport-backed SSH access and are more ergonomic than a plain SFTP
session. If `rsync` / `rclone` hit issues — they wrap OpenSSH and don't tolerate every
intermediate config — fall back to an `sftp` client directly.

Large transfers go through the SFTP endpoint `xfer.dcai.dk`, which requires your public IP to be
whitelisted. Contact [DCAI support](mailto:support@dcai.dk) and share the output of
`curl ifconfig.io` to request access.

```text file=~/.ssh/config
Host gefion-xfer
  Port 2234
  User <username>
  HostName xfer.dcai.dk
```

```ini file=~/.config/rclone/rclone.conf
[gefion]
disable_hashcheck = true
shell_type = unix
ssh = ssh gefion-xfer
type = sftp
```

#### Inbound (push to Gefion)

Small data — `rsync` over the existing `gefion` SSH host:

```bash
rsync -avz ./src gefion:~/project/src
```

Large datasets — `rclone` via the SFTP endpoint:

```bash
rclone sync ./datasets gefion:to_gefion/dest --dry-run   # preview changes
rclone sync ./datasets gefion:to_gefion/dest             # sync for real
rclone ls gefion:to_gefion/dest                          # list files
```

<Aside type="note">

Data uploaded via the SFTP endpoint lands on a transfer staging area, not directly in your home
directory. After the transfer, copy from the staging host on the login node — see the DCAI guide
for the current staging host and path.

</Aside>

#### Outbound (pull from Gefion)

Same tools, in reverse — run from your local machine pulling from `gefion:...`:

```bash
rsync -avz gefion:~/project/results ./results
```

```bash
rclone sync gefion:from_gefion/results ./results
```

#### Between clusters

For large inter-cluster transfers, the recommended bridge is via the DTU transfer node. All P1
clusters and most Danish university systems are on the
[Danish Research Network (forskningsnettet)](https://www.deic.dk/en/danish-research-network),
which gives high-bandwidth, low-latency links between Danish research institutions — going
cluster → cluster over forskningsnettet is much faster than round-tripping via your local machine
over the public internet.

From inside Gefion, SFTP into `transfer.gbar.dtu.dk`, then SSH into the DTU P1 cluster from there
and move the data further. Use the `sftp` client directly for this hop — `rsync` and `rclone`
wrap OpenSSH and currently don't traverse this path cleanly.

```bash
# from inside Gefion
sftp <dtu-username>@transfer.gbar.dtu.dk
# then SSH into your DTU account and pull from /tmp or your transfer landing dir
```

#### SFTP fallback

If `rsync` / `rclone` aren't working, connect with the SFTP client directly:

```bash
sftp gefion-xfer
```

<details>
<summary>Tip: Using the DTU cluster as a jump host</summary>

If your IP cannot be whitelisted on `xfer.dcai.dk` but you do have DTU access, you can use the
DTU cluster as a proxy. Add these entries:

```text file=~/.ssh/config
Host dtu-transfer
  ForwardAgent yes
  User <dtu-username>
  HostName transfer.gbar.dtu.dk
  ControlMaster no
  ControlPersist no

Host gefion-jump
  Port 2234
  User <gefion-username>
  HostName xfer.dcai.dk
  ProxyJump dtu-transfer
```

```ini file=~/.config/rclone/rclone.conf
[gefion-jump]
disable_hashcheck = true
shell_type = unix
ssh = ssh gefion-jump
type = sftp
```

Then use `gefion-jump:` instead of `gefion:` in your rclone commands.

</details>

### How to start an interactive job

Request an interactive session with GPUs via `srun`:

```bash
ssh gefion
srun --pty -N 1 -n 1 --gpus=2 --mem=8GB --time=1:00:00 /bin/bash
```

Which will open an interactive session with 2 H100 GPUs and 8 GB of RAM.

### How to submit a batch job

```bash file=train.sh
#!/bin/bash

#SBATCH --account=<account-nb>
#SBATCH --job-name=testing-train-script
#SBATCH --nodes=1
#SBATCH --gpus=2
#SBATCH --mem=20GB
#SBATCH --time=1:00:00
#SBATCH --error=slurm_files/error/%x_%j.err
#SBATCH --output=slurm_files/output/%x_%j.out

cd $SLURM_SUBMIT_DIR
module load CUDA/12.8
nvidia-smi
uv run train.py
```

```bash
sbatch train.sh              # submit above script 1 time
sbatch --array=0-7 train.sh  # submit above script 8 times
```

## Policy & Support

### Usage Rules

- Per-person limit of 2 GPUs, up to 16 H100 GPUs total across the sandbox
- 300 TB shared storage allowance
- User accounts are individual and non-transferable
- Accounts can be associated with one or more Gefion projects

Limits may be adjusted on an ongoing basis to meet the needs of P1 students and faculty.

